  "title": "Simple negative roots for the rows of the Delannoy matrix square",
  "batch_label": "primary-20260914",
  "kind": "result-note",
  "original_status": "unresolved",
  "scope": "Let D(n,k) count paths from (0,0) to (n-k,k) using steps (1,0), (0,1), and (1,1), with D(n,k)=0 outside 0<=k<=n and the empty path counted once. For the ordinary matrix square define G_n(x)=sum_{k=0}^n sum_{i=k}^n D(n,i)D(i,k)x^k. Then G_0=1, and for every integer n>=1, G_n is monic of degree n and has n distinct real roots, all in the open interval (-4-3*sqrt(2), -3+2*sqrt(2)). This claim concerns only D^2; it does not assert the A^2 part of the frozen original or consecutive interlacing.",
  "gate_basis": "Current released terminal, manifest, PDF, TeX freeze, snapshot files, provenance and four audits agree.",
